// src/net_tls_verify.pdx -- libpdx-net.M3-003 (libpdx-net#13)
//
// Ed25519 transcript-signature verification -- scaffold-only,
// R100-PREP-001 + R100-PREP-005 blocked.
//
// =============================================================================
// BLOCKERS (read this first)
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is SCAFFOLD-ONLY. It performs NO cryptographic
// verification of any kind. Two separate, independently-tracked prep
// items must close before the real body can land:
//
//   R100-PREP-001 -- TLS trust pinning (KIND_TLS_TRUST).
//     The pinned-key trust anchor that tells this function WHICH
//     public key a given server is allowed to present (design/
//     networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md §0.5's pinned-key trust
//     model). It does not exist kernel-side yet; pdxtrust M2 mints
//     the real caps. Until it does, `pubkey_ptr` is a raw caller-
//     supplied pointer with NO provenance -- nothing in this tree can
//     say where that key came from, so even a real Ed25519 verify
//     against it would answer the wrong question ("does this
//     signature match this key" rather than "is this the key this
//     host is pinned to"). See caps.decl's KIND_TLS_TRUST forward
//     reference.
//
//   R100-PREP-005 -- paideia-as crypto FFI thunks.
//     The extern-C thunk layer that makes a paideia-as crypto
//     primitive reachable from `.pdx` source. See the "not linkable"
//     finding below.
//
// =============================================================================
// SCOPE (design/networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md §2.4 + §12.4)
// =============================================================================
//
// `net_tls_verify_transcript(pubkey_ptr, sig_ptr, sig_len,
// transcript_ptr, transcript_len) -> u64` is the M3-003 call site
// for RFC 8446 §4.4.3 CertificateVerify: given the server's
// (pinned) Ed25519 public key, the 64-byte detached signature the
// server sent, and the handshake transcript those bytes are claimed
// to cover, decide whether the signature verifies.
//
// Per §2.4 ("prefer Ed25519 over ML-DSA-65 for v1") Ed25519 is the
// ONLY signature algorithm this milestone implements. The
// MLDSA65_RESERVED tag net_tls_record.pdx already reserves in
// TlsHandshakeRecord@0.1's `sig_algo` enum exists so a future
// ML-DSA-65 verify path is an additive landing rather than a
// schema break; nothing here verifies ML-DSA-65 today.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why this is a WEAK stub: `paideia_crypto_ed25519_verify` is NOT linkable
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The wave dispatch for this issue asks to call the paideia-as
// Ed25519 verify intrinsic "if it is externally linkable from this
// repo's toolchain, else a WEAK stub". Checking the live paideia-as
// tree resolves that conditional to "not linkable", on two
// independent counts:
//
//   1. No extern-C thunk. Ed25519 verify DID land as real,
//      test-vector-backed Rust: `paideia_as_crypto::curve::ed25519::
//      ed25519_verify(public: &[u8;32], msg: &[u8], signature:
//      &[u8;64]) -> bool`, re-exported at the crate root. But the
//      FFI layer that flattens a primitive into a `#[unsafe(
//      no_mangle)] pub extern "C" fn` -- `paideia-as-crypto::ffi` --
//      contains exactly three primitives' thunks today:
//      `ffi/argon2id.rs`, `ffi/chacha20_poly1305.rs`,
//      `ffi/ml_kem_768.rs`. There is no `ffi/ed25519.rs` and no
//      `paideia_crypto_ed25519_verify` symbol anywhere in that
//      module. A `.pdx` `call paideia_crypto_ed25519_verify` would
//      emit a CALL relocation nothing in the link ever satisfies.
//
//   2. No stdlib lowering recipe. The OTHER (and, per
//      tools/user/libpdx-volume/src/pdxb_crypto.pdx, the sanctioned)
//      way `.pdx` source reaches a crypto primitive is a module-local
//      `trait` declaration whose call site
//      `stdlib_lowering::cryptoops` rewrites into the extern-C CALL.
//      That dispatch table also covers exactly Argon2id /
//      ChaCha20Poly1305 / MlKem768 / MlDsa65 -- there is no `Ed25519`
//      arm, so a module-local `trait Ed25519 { fn verify(...) }`
//      declared here would fail to elaborate rather than link.
//
// This is the same R100-PREP-005 shape net_tls_key_schedule.pdx
// already documents for SHA-256/HKDF: the primitive is LANDED and
// TESTED inside paideia-as, and is simply not reachable from `.pdx`.
// Note the contrast with ChaCha20-Poly1305, which DOES have both a
// thunk and a lowering recipe and is therefore called for real in
// net_tls_record.pdx's M3-004 body -- "R100-PREP-005" is not a
// single switch, it is a per-primitive FFI-exposure gap, and this
// primitive is on the wrong side of it.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FAIL-CLOSED, deliberately: this stub does NOT return success
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The issue text for this milestone suggests a stub that "returns
// success (0) unconditionally". This file does NOT do that, and the
// deviation is deliberate and load-bearing.
//
// A signature verifier is the one function in a TLS stack where a
// fail-OPEN stub is indistinguishable from a backdoor: any caller
// wired to it before the real body lands would accept EVERY server
// key, including a rogue one, while the audit trail
// (TlsHandshakeRecord@0.1) happily stamps VERDICT_OK. That is
// precisely the outcome §0.5's pinned-key trust model exists to
// prevent, and precisely the signal net_tls_record.pdx's own header
// calls "the LOAD-BEARING audit signal".
//
// Every other stub in this repository is fail-closed
// (net_tls_key_schedule.pdx zero-fills and returns NOT_IMPL;
// net_api.pdx's stubs return NOT_IMPL; net_http_request.pdx's
// encoders return a bit-63-tagged NOT_IMPL). This one follows that
// established convention: it returns TLSV_ERR_NOT_IMPL (0x2F, ==
// NetTypes.TLS_ERR_NOT_IMPL) on every path that reaches the
// would-be verify. A caller that maps "nonzero" onto
// TLSR_VERDICT_KEY_MISMATCH gets the safe answer by default; a
// caller that requires a real verdict finds out at the first call
// rather than in production.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What the stub DOES do: argument-shape validation
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The stub is not inert. It runs the same argument-shape checks the
// real body needs, so the checks are exercised from day one and the
// swap does not have to introduce them alongside the crypto:
//
//   * pubkey_ptr / sig_ptr / transcript_ptr must all be non-NULL.
//   * sig_len must be EXACTLY TLSV_SIG_BYTES (64) -- an Ed25519
//     signature is R(32) || S(32) per RFC 8032 §3.3. This is the
//     length constant most likely to be got wrong by a caller that
//     conflates it with the 32-byte PUBLIC key length, so it is
//     checked explicitly rather than inferred.
//
// A shape failure returns TLSV_ERR_INVAL (0x01, ==
// NetTypes.NET_ERR_INVAL) -- distinguishable from
// TLSV_ERR_NOT_IMPL, so a caller can tell "you called me wrong"
// from "this primitive does not exist yet". Reusing the closest-fit
// existing enum value rather than minting a parallel one is this
// repo's established precedent (net_dns_query.pdx's DNSQ_ERR_ENCODE
// reuse of DNS_ERR_MALFORMED; net_tls_key_schedule.pdx's
// TKS_ERR_NOT_IMPL reuse of TLS_ERR_NOT_IMPL).
//
// transcript_len is deliberately NOT range-checked: a zero-length
// transcript is a legal (if useless) Ed25519 message per RFC 8032,
// and the real body passes it straight through to the primitive.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// SWAP SITE (what the real body looks like once both preps close)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The swap is mechanical and contained to this file. Once
// `paideia-as-crypto::ffi` grows an Ed25519 thunk AND
// `stdlib_lowering::cryptoops` grows the matching dispatch arm,
// this module adds -- following tools/user/libpdx-volume/src/
// pdxb_crypto.pdx verbatim, which is the proven working template
// for reaching a crypto intrinsic from a `.pdx` library in this org:
//
//   trait Ed25519 {
//     fn verify(pubkey_ptr: u64, msg_ptr: u64, msg_len: u64,
//               sig_ptr: u64) -> i64 !{crypto} @{paideia.crypto};
//   }
//
//   pub let net_tls_verify_ed25519_raw
//     : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> i64 !{crypto} @{paideia.crypto} =
//     fn (pubkey_ptr: u64, msg_ptr: u64, msg_len: u64, sig_ptr: u64) ->
//       Ed25519::verify(pubkey_ptr, msg_ptr, msg_len, sig_ptr)
//
// (the plain-lambda tail-expression body is REQUIRED -- the
// stdlib-lowering recipe only fires on a call expression at a
// lambda-body position, it is not reachable from inside an `unsafe`
// asm block).
//
// `net_tls_verify_transcript`'s own body then keeps its existing
// shape checks, marshals (pubkey_ptr, transcript_ptr,
// transcript_len, sig_ptr) into rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx, calls the wrapper,
// and maps the result: 0 -> TLSV_OK, anything else ->
// TLSV_ERR_KEY_MISMATCH. Its signature, its return-code domain, and
// every call site are unaffected -- callers do not re-link.
//
// Note what the swap does NOT fix: R100-PREP-001. Even with a real
// Ed25519 verify, `pubkey_ptr` still has no provenance until
// KIND_TLS_TRUST lands. The honest end state is a THIRD landing in
// which net_tls_wrap.pdx resolves a trust cap to a pinned key and
// hands THAT pointer here.
//
// =============================================================================
// paideia-as compliance
// =============================================================================
//
//   - Module name PascalCase basename (`NetTlsVerify`) matches the
//     file basename (net_tls_verify.pdx), per this repo's convention.
//   - Labels prefixed `libpdxnet_tlsv_` -- disjoint from every other
//     label prefix in this repo, and free of the reserved keywords
//     (`loop`, `if`, ...) the parser claims.
//   - No `test rN, rN` -- `cmp reg, 0` + je/jne only.
//   - Every immediate here is a small imm32 (0, 64, 0x01, 0x2F) --
//     no MOVABS staging needed, and no `cmp reg, imm64` anywhere
//     (the wide-immediate compare must always stage through a
//     register; there is no such compare in this file).
//   - Leaf function: no nested `call`, so no callee-save prologue
//     and no rsp alignment padding is required at all.
//   - No multiplies -- no `imul` in any form.
//   - String literals: none.

module NetTlsVerify = structure {

  // === Ed25519 wire sizes (RFC 8032 §5.1) ===================================
  //
  // These two are the constants a caller is most likely to swap by
  // accident. A public key is 32 bytes (a compressed Edwards point);
  // a signature is 64 bytes (R, a compressed point, concatenated
  // with S, a 32-byte scalar). Neither carries a length prefix or a
  // tag byte -- there is no off-by-one to make here, the sizes are
  // exact and absolute.
  pub let TLSV_PUBKEY_BYTES : u64 = 32
  pub let TLSV_SIG_BYTES    : u64 = 64

  // === Return-code domain ===================================================
  //
  // Every value is folded through the closest-fit existing NetTypes
  // enum value rather than minting a parallel domain -- this repo's
  // established precedent. TLSV_OK is unreachable at this scaffold
  // landing (nothing verifies) but is published so callers can write
  // the `cmp rax, TLSV_OK`-shaped check today and not re-link when
  // the real body lands.
  pub let TLSV_OK              : u64 = 0x20   // == NetTypes.TLS_OK
  pub let TLSV_ERR_KEY_MISMATCH : u64 = 0x21  // == NetTypes.TLS_ERR_KEY_MISMATCH
                                               //    (real body: sig did not
                                               //     verify under pubkey)
  pub let TLSV_ERR_INVAL       : u64 = 0x01   // == NetTypes.NET_ERR_INVAL
                                               //    (caller arg shape wrong)
  pub let TLSV_ERR_NOT_IMPL    : u64 = 0x2F   // == NetTypes.TLS_ERR_NOT_IMPL
                                               //    (scaffold -- no verify)

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_tls_verify_transcript(pubkey_ptr, sig_ptr, sig_len,
  //                           transcript_ptr, transcript_len) -> u64
  //
  // WEAK, FAIL-CLOSED scaffold. See the file header for (a) the
  // confirmed absence of any linkable Ed25519 path from `.pdx`,
  // (b) the R100-PREP-001 / R100-PREP-005 blockers, and (c) why
  // this refuses rather than returning success.
  //
  // Returns:
  //   TLSV_ERR_INVAL    (0x01) -- a NULL pointer argument, or
  //                               sig_len != 64.
  //   TLSV_ERR_NOT_IMPL (0x2F) -- arguments are well-shaped, but no
  //                               verification was performed and NO
  //                               TRUST DECISION MAY BE INFERRED.
  //
  // It never returns TLSV_OK at this landing. A caller must treat a
  // non-TLSV_OK result as "not verified" and stamp
  // TLSR_VERDICT_KEY_MISMATCH on its TlsHandshakeRecord@0.1.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_tls_verify_transcript :
    (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (pubkey_ptr: u64, sig_ptr: u64, sig_len: u64,
        transcript_ptr: u64, transcript_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M3-003 (#13): WEAK fail-closed scaffold for RFC 8446 §4.4.3 CertificateVerify. See the file header for the confirmed finding that NEITHER route to a crypto primitive from `.pdx` reaches Ed25519 -- `paideia-as-crypto::ffi` has thunks for Argon2id/ChaCha20-Poly1305/ML-KEM-768 only (no `paideia_crypto_ed25519_verify` symbol exists to `call`), and `stdlib_lowering::cryptoops` has no `Ed25519` dispatch arm (so a module-local `trait Ed25519` would fail to elaborate) -- plus the R100-PREP-001 (KIND_TLS_TRUST pubkey provenance) and R100-PREP-005 (FFI thunk) blockers. Body runs the real body's own argument-shape gates so the swap adds only crypto: `cmp rdi,0; je bad` (pubkey_ptr), `cmp rsi,0; je bad` (sig_ptr), `cmp rcx,0; je bad` (transcript_ptr), `cmp rdx,64; jne bad` (sig_len must be exactly TLSV_SIG_BYTES -- R||S per RFC 8032 §3.3; the constant a caller most often confuses with the 32-byte pubkey length). r8=transcript_len is accepted for future-signature stability and deliberately unchecked (a zero-length message is legal for Ed25519). Falls through to `mov rax,0x2F; ret` (TLSV_ERR_NOT_IMPL) -- deliberately NOT success: a fail-open signature verifier is indistinguishable from a backdoor, and every other stub in this repo is fail-closed. bad path returns 0x01 (TLSV_ERR_INVAL, == NetTypes.NET_ERR_INVAL) so 'called wrong' is distinguishable from 'not implemented'. All four immediates (0, 64, 0x2F, 0x01) are small imm32 -- no MOVABS, and no `cmp reg,imm64` anywhere. Uses cmp/je/jne, never `test rN,rN`. Leaf -- no nested call, so no callee-save prologue and no rsp%16 padding. No memory access on any path. Labels prefixed libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_bad;
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je  libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_bad;
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je  libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_bad;
        cmp rdx, 64;
        jne libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_bad;

        mov rax, 0x2F;                   // TLSV_ERR_NOT_IMPL -- fail-closed
        ret;

      libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_bad:
        mov rax, 0x01;                   // TLSV_ERR_INVAL
        ret
      }
    }

}
